WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.088
Embedded devices communicate through
network stacks, radio links,

2
00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:07.818
serial interfaces, field buses, sensors,
actuators, storage parts,

3
00:00:07.898 --> 00:00:09.197
and peripheral drivers.

4
00:00:09.277 --> 00:00:13.422
Each path can bring data into the device
or cause the device to perform an action.

5
00:00:13.502 --> 00:00:16.533
Communication handlers should have
explicit contracts.

6
00:00:16.613 --> 00:00:20.572
A handler should validate message size,
source expectation, command type,

7
00:00:20.652 --> 00:00:23.559
version, state, and timing assumptions
before acting.

8
00:00:23.639 --> 00:00:26.856
It should also define what happens on
timeout, partial input,

9
00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:30.215
repeated messages, unsupported values, and
resource limits.

10
00:00:30.295 --> 00:00:33.264
Clear contracts make handlers easier to
test and review.

11
00:00:33.344 --> 00:00:37.675
Peripheral inputs should not be trusted
merely because they are local to the board.

12
00:00:37.755 --> 00:00:40.724
A sensor can be disconnected, noisy,
stale, out of range,

13
00:00:40.804 --> 00:00:43.216
or inconsistent with the current device
mode.

14
00:00:43.296 --> 00:00:45.524
A bus device can report unexpected status.

15
00:00:45.604 --> 00:00:49.377
A driver should normalize hardware details
and report errors in a way the

16
00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:51.127
application can handle safely.

17
00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:54.053
DMA, shared buffers, RTOS tasks, interrupt
handlers,

18
00:00:54.133 --> 00:00:57.906
and driver boundaries require careful
ownership and synchronization.

19
00:00:57.986 --> 00:01:00.708
The design should state when hardware owns
a buffer,

20
00:01:00.788 --> 00:01:03.696
when software may read it, which task owns
shared state,

21
00:01:03.776 --> 00:01:06.993
and which synchronization rule protects
concurrent access.

22
00:01:07.073 --> 00:01:10.228
Ambiguous ownership is a reliability and
security problem.

23
00:01:10.308 --> 00:01:14.576
Least privilege can apply inside firmware
even when the platform does not provide

24
00:01:14.656 --> 00:01:15.955
full process isolation.

25
00:01:16.035 --> 00:01:19.932
Separate roles, limit exposed commands,
keep high-authority code small,

26
00:01:20.012 --> 00:01:22.672
restrict direct register access to driver
layers,

27
00:01:22.752 --> 00:01:26.093
and avoid giving every task access to
every resource by default.

28
00:01:26.173 --> 00:01:29.019
Isolation choices should match platform
capability.

29
00:01:29.099 --> 00:01:32.996
An MPU, MMU, RTOS task boundary, hardware
privilege mode, secure element,

30
00:01:33.076 --> 00:01:36.478
or peripheral access rule can support the
design when available.

31
00:01:36.558 --> 00:01:38.600
These features are not magic switches;

32
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.134
they work best when the software
architecture already defines roles and boundaries.

33
00:01:43.214 --> 00:01:45.874
Review communications and peripherals
together.

34
00:01:45.954 --> 00:01:50.222
The most important question is not whether
data arrived through a network or a wire

35
00:01:50.302 --> 00:01:50.983
on the board.

36
00:01:51.063 --> 00:01:54.713
The question is what the firmware assumes
about that data, who owns it,

37
00:01:54.793 --> 00:01:57.700
how it is validated, and what state
changes it may cause.
